WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
The microglia in your brain were not made by your brain.

2
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:13.680
In mice, most of them descend from cells born in the yolk sac, a membrane outside

3
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.460
the embryo, before the brain had a blood-brain barrier.

4
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.520
Glia is a job description, not a family.

5
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.200
So where does each kind of glial cell come from?

6
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:28.263
Lineage methods gave the answer.

7
00:00:30.203 --> 00:00:35.203
Astrocytes, oligodendrocytes and ependymal cells come from the neuroepithelium of

8
00:00:35.203 --> 00:00:39.683
the neural tube, mostly through the radial glia that also made the neurons.

9
00:00:40.763 --> 00:00:42.583
Microglia come from the yolk sac.

10
00:00:43.623 --> 00:00:48.383
Schwann cells and satellite glia come from the neural crest, the cells that leave

11
00:00:48.383 --> 00:00:50.483
the edges of the closing neural tube.

12
00:00:51.343 --> 00:00:53.423
Three birthplaces, one name.

13
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:56.377
Begin in the neural tube.

14
00:00:57.137 --> 00:01:03.177
In the mouse cortex, radial glia make neurons first, and most astrocytes appear late.

15
00:01:04.037 --> 00:01:06.277
The same progenitors change their output.

16
00:01:07.197 --> 00:01:13.417
In cultured rodent cortical progenitors, the cytokine CNTF drives cells to make

17
00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:16.337
GFAP, the standard astrocyte marker.

18
00:01:16.957 --> 00:01:19.297
It acts through JAK and STAT3.

19
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:26.537
Early on, the STAT3 site in the GFAP promoter is methylated, so the cell cannot read

20
00:01:26.537 --> 00:01:29.717
it. Later the site is demethylated.

21
00:01:30.597 --> 00:01:35.857
One proposal is that newborn neurons make a different cytokine, cardiotrophin-1.

22
00:01:37.547 --> 00:01:39.967
Oligodendrocytes start in a ventral strip.

23
00:01:40.707 --> 00:01:45.647
In the spinal cord, Sonic hedgehog from the floor plate divides the ventral cord

24
00:01:45.647 --> 00:01:47.007
into progenitor domains.

25
00:01:47.927 --> 00:01:51.447
One of them, pMN, expresses Olig2.

26
00:01:52.227 --> 00:01:58.607
In mice and chicks, pMN first makes motor neurons, then oligodendrocyte precursors.

27
00:01:58.607 --> 00:02:05.427
Mice lacking both Olig1 and Olig2 have no spinal motor neurons and no spinal

28
00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:06.547
oligodendrocytes.

29
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.880
Microglia are the odd ones out.

30
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:16.820
To trace them, researchers gave mice one dose of tamoxifen at about embryonic day 7.5.

31
00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:22.040
That permanently marked yolk-sac cells and all their descendants.

32
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.500
In adults, about a third of microglia carried the mark.

33
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.780
Very few blood cells did.

34
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.960
Embryos without a heartbeat made yolk-sac macrophages but no brain microglia.

35
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.680
So the progenitors travel in through the early circulation.

36
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:44.163
In the peripheral nervous system, glia come from the neural crest.

37
00:02:45.323 --> 00:02:51.003
Schwann cells pass through steps: crest cell, then Schwann cell precursor, then

38
00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:52.403
immature Schwann cell.

39
00:02:53.323 --> 00:02:57.043
In culture, the precursors die unless neurons are present.

40
00:02:58.103 --> 00:03:01.483
Neuregulin, a growth factor on axons, can keep them alive.

41
00:03:02.783 --> 00:03:07.823
Mice without the receptor ErbB3 lack precursors along their nerves.

42
00:03:07.823 --> 00:03:12.863
So ask where a glial cell was born, and which signal it needs.
